  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "OMNY is the MTA's modern, contactless tap-and-go payment system (via smartphone, wearable, or smart card) replacing the swipe-based MetroCard, which is being phased out by the end of 2025. OMNY offers benefits like automatic 7-day fare capping (12 rides), while MetroCard requires upfront payment for unlimited passes. Key Comparisons: Payment Method: OMNY uses NFC (contactless credit cards, phone wallets, or dedicated OMNY cards). MetroCard requires a magnetic stripe swipe. Fare Capping: OMNY offers a 7-day fare cap: after 12 rides in a week (starting Monday), all subsequent rides are free. MetroCard requires purchasing a 7-Day or 30-Day unlimited pass upfront. Transfers: Both systems offer free transfers between subways and buses. Accessibility & Convenience: OMNY reduces long lines at vending machines and allows for faster entry, though some users report issues with incorrectly calculated caps or denied entries, say users on Facebook. Replacement Status: MetroCard is being phased out, with sales ending at the end of 2025. When to use which: Use OMNY: If you use a smartphone or contactless card and want to pay-as-you-go without upfront costs. Use MetroCard: Only if you have not yet switched or prefer the familiarity, though you will need to switch by late 2025.",
